  "question": {
    "text": "Two large plane mirrors&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PM</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>PN</mi></math> are arrange as shown. The length of the part of large screen <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>SS</mi><mo>'</mo></math> in which two images of the object placed at&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></math> can be seen is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math> (in m). Find the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>x.</mi></math><br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\qt0105_69e11a21ea9a.png\" style=\"width: 215px; height: 200px;\" />"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0105_65e97e7cd8ef.png\" style=\"width: 350px; height: 340px;\" /></p><p>The normals for the mirror PM is PS&#39; and the normal to the mirror PN is PS. From the geometry of the figure it can be seen that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>SS</mi><mo>'</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>.</p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  }
}